{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-34b91daa1b63463bb486fd6e8e0644d5","ordinal":241,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.241","text":"\n\nJudging from these and similar points, then, we should say that the Methodists’ way in medicine has a certain affinity with skepticism, more than the other medical schools have. Even so, this holds only in comparison with them, not without qualification. Having said this much about the approaches that seem to lie close to the skeptics’ way, we here complete both the general account of skepticism and the first book of the Outlines."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-18e6e97bd0694c9eae6be552b7e59f9a","ordinal":242,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.1","text":"\n\nSince we have taken up the investigation against the dogmatists, let us go through each of the parts of what is called philosophy, concisely and in outline. First, though, we must reply to those who keep repeating that the skeptic cannot investigate, or even think at all, about the things they hold beliefs about."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-59581716edd144a3846d69a7eaa08bda","ordinal":243,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.2","text":"\n\nThey say that the skeptic either apprehends what the dogmatists say or does not apprehend it. If he apprehends it, how could he be puzzled about things he says he has apprehended? If he does not apprehend it, then he does not even know how to speak about things he has not apprehended."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5664b8b90ffe431bbfa9c5286f4f755e","ordinal":244,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.3","text":"\n\nSomeone who does not know, say, what the “by subtraction” theorem or the “through two conditionals” theorem is cannot say anything about them either. In the same way, someone who does not know each of the things the dogmatists say cannot investigate against them about things he does not know. So the skeptic is in no way able to investigate what the dogmatists say.\n〔1〕 Two theorems of Stoic logic; the Greek text of their names is uncertain."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0d0450d0348f47898aed028a1750c1e3","ordinal":245,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.4","text":"\n\nLet those who say this answer us: in what sense are they using the word “apprehending” here? Do they mean simply thinking, without also firmly affirming the reality of the things we are discussing? Or do they mean thinking together with positing the reality of the things we are discussing? Suppose they say that in their argument apprehending is assenting to an apprehensive impression. The apprehensive impression is one that comes from something real, imprinted and stamped in accordance with that real thing itself, of such a kind as could not come from something unreal. In that case perhaps not even they will want it to be impossible to investigate things they have not apprehended in this way."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ddd227c6933a4f85bbbe036f70e161ee","ordinal":246,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.5","text":"\n\nFor example, suppose the Stoic investigates against the Epicurean, who says that substance is divided, or that god does not exercise providence over things in the world, or that pleasure is good. Has the Stoic apprehended these claims or not? If he has apprehended them, then in saying they are real he does away with the Stoa altogether. If he has not, he cannot say anything against them."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fc84be9976004e59b52ecdd7b5a784de","ordinal":247,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.6","text":"\n\nThe same must be said to the adherents of the other schools whenever they want to investigate the views of those who think differently. So they cannot investigate anything with one another. Or rather, if we are not to talk nonsense, practically their whole dogmatism will be thrown into confusion, and the skeptical philosophy will be firmly established, once it is granted that one cannot investigate what has not been apprehended in this way."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-db13fd8fadd94ecea1bfe867092b5172","ordinal":248,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.7","text":"\n\nTake someone who makes assertions and holds beliefs about some non-evident matter. He will say that he makes assertions about it either having apprehended it or without having apprehended it. If he has not apprehended it, he will not be credible. If he has apprehended it, he will say that he apprehended it in one of two ways. Either it presented itself to him directly, of itself and clearly, or he reached it through some inquiry and investigation."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b5ca11303ba1408baf7f19032295dc6c","ordinal":249,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.8","text":"\n\nSuppose he says that the non-evident thing presented itself to him directly, of itself and clearly, through his encountering it, and was apprehended. Then it would not be non-evident at all. It would be apparent to everyone equally, agreed upon and undisputed. But about each non-evident thing an endless disagreement has arisen among them. So the dogmatist who firmly affirms and makes assertions about the reality of a non-evident thing cannot have apprehended it by its presenting itself to him of itself and clearly."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f1d0b662ec4848939c4933ffedd41b4d","ordinal":250,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.9","text":"\n\nIf he reached it through some inquiry, how was he able to investigate it before he had precisely apprehended it, on the hypothesis before us? Investigation requires that what is to be investigated first be precisely apprehended, and only then investigated. Apprehension of the thing under investigation, in turn, requires that it has in every case been investigated beforehand. So by the mode of reciprocity it becomes impossible for them either to investigate non-evident things or to hold beliefs about them. If some of them want to start from apprehension, we bring them round to the point that they must have investigated the thing before apprehending it. If they want to start from investigation, we bring them round to the point that they must have apprehended what is to be investigated before investigating it. So for these reasons they can neither apprehend any non-evident thing nor make firm assertions about such things. From this it will follow directly, I think, that the dogmatists’ clever inventions are done away with and the suspensive philosophy is brought in."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a8de4672e4f4453ebf118ad4de1c9975","ordinal":251,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.10","text":"\n\nThey may say instead that the kind of apprehension they think must precede investigation is not this kind but simply thinking. In that case it is not impossible for those who suspend judgment about the reality of non-evident things to investigate. The skeptic is not, I think, barred from thinking. Thinking arises from things that strike him passively ⟨and⟩ from arguments that appear to him, and it does not in every case bring with it the reality of what is thought. For we think not only of real things, as they say, but also of unreal ones. So the person who suspends judgment remains in the skeptical disposition both while investigating and while thinking. We have already shown that he assents to what strikes him by way of a passive impression, insofar as it appears to him."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d7253cbd8a824e13ac2daf4a255f3ceb","ordinal":252,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.11","text":"\n\nConsider whether it is not rather the dogmatists who are barred from investigation even now. Going on investigating things is not inconsistent for those who admit that they do not know how things are in their nature. It is inconsistent for those who think they know them precisely. For the latter, investigation has already reached its end, as they suppose. For the former, the reason why any investigation is undertaken is still present: the thought that they have not found the answer."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-46e4f3b1eb7647bbb72b16834af777d0","ordinal":253,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.12","text":"\n\nSo for the present we must investigate each part of what is called philosophy, concisely. There has been much disagreement among the dogmatists about the parts of philosophy: some say there is one, some two, some three. It would not be appropriate to go into this further. We will set out, even-handedly, the view of those who seem to have dealt with the matter more thoroughly, and conduct our argument in line with it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-39e97ff061a849078f03538ad28e4bc5","ordinal":254,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.13","text":"\n\nThe Stoics, then, and some others say that there are three parts of philosophy: logic, physics and ethics. They begin their teaching with logic, although there has been much dispute about where one ought to begin. We will follow them, without holding opinions. What is said in the three parts requires judgment and a criterion, and the account of the criterion seems to be included in the logical part. So let us begin with the account of the criterion and with the logical part,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-eee8e7c1e1a942499e272b781b5f1976","ordinal":255,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.14","text":"\n\nafter first saying this. “Criterion” is used both of that by which, they say, reality and unreality are judged, and of that which we attend to in living. Our task now is to discuss what is said to be the criterion of truth. We went through the criterion in the other sense in our account of skepticism."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-78593a547f5c4745a381c5e00b1c006b","ordinal":256,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.15","text":"\n\nThe criterion under discussion, then, is spoken of in three ways: in a general, a special and a most special sense. In the general sense it is any standard of apprehension, and in this sense even natural things, like sight, are called criteria. In the special sense it is any technical standard of apprehension, like a ruler or a pair of compasses. In the most special sense it is any standard of apprehension of a non-evident matter. In this sense the things of everyday life are not called criteria. Only logical criteria are, the ones the dogmatists bring forward for judging truth."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fccd5fea040b4922b1e2f05775275b80","ordinal":257,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.16","text":"\n\nSo we propose to go through the logical criterion primarily. But the logical criterion too might be spoken of in three ways: the criterion “by which,” “through which” and “according to which.” For instance, the “by which” is a human being. The “through which” is either perception or the intellect. The “according to which” is the impact of the impression, according to which the human being sets about judging through one of the things just mentioned."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d53ff6dcb54641bfaabbaf0213d05947","ordinal":258,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.17","text":"\n\nIt was perhaps appropriate to say this first, so that we have a conception of what our argument is about. Now let us proceed to our rebuttal of those who rashly say they have apprehended the criterion of truth, beginning with the disagreement."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-46ccdd1fbb4d4ff3ae1198b415edf7e1","ordinal":259,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.18","text":"\n\nOf those who have discussed the criterion, some have asserted that there is one, like the Stoics and some others. Others have asserted that there is not. Among them are Xeniades of Corinth and Xenophanes of Colophon, who says: “but opinion is fashioned over all things.” We have suspended judgment as to whether there is one or not."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-bf1fe7b2c4cc47f0bb973de4e86e6bba","ordinal":260,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.19","text":"\n\nThey will say, then, that this disagreement is either decidable or undecidable. If it is undecidable, they will grant at once that one must suspend judgment. If it is decidable, let them say what it will be judged by. We have no agreed criterion. We do not even know whether there is one at all; we are still investigating."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-72a05df52f824bf6981c4f2f875441d5","ordinal":261,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.20","text":"\n\nAgain, for the disagreement about the criterion to be decided, we must have an agreed criterion by which we can judge it. And for us to have an agreed criterion, the disagreement about the criterion must first be decided. With the argument falling into the mode of reciprocity in this way, finding the criterion becomes impossible. We do not allow them to adopt a criterion by hypothesis, and if they want to judge the criterion by a criterion, we throw them into an infinite regress. Further, since a proof needs a proven criterion and a criterion needs a judged proof, they are thrown into the mode of reciprocity."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b2d298e1d4504747a76eea55f7cac1c3","ordinal":262,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.21","text":"\n\nWe think these points too are enough to show the rashness of the dogmatists in their account of the criterion. Still, so that we can refute them in various ways, it is not out of place to dwell on the topic. We do not propose to contend with each of the views about the criterion in detail. The disagreement has become unspeakably large, and that way we too would necessarily fall into an unmethodical argument. But the criterion we are investigating seems to be threefold: the “by which,” the “through which” and the “according to which.” So we will go through each of these in turn and establish its inapprehensibility, and in this way our argument will be both methodical and complete. Let us begin with the “by which,” for the others seem somehow to become puzzling along with it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-bd3dca40e92a4df781a3539bd5da634a","ordinal":263,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.22","text":"\n\nThe human being, it seems to me, is not only inapprehensible but even inconceivable, as far as what the dogmatists say goes. At any rate, we hear Plato’s Socrates explicitly admitting that he does not know whether he is a human being or something else. And when they want to establish the concept of a human being, first they disagree, and then they say unintelligible things.\n〔1〕 In Plato’s Phaedrus (230a) Socrates says he is investigating whether he is a beast more complex than Typhon or a gentler, simpler creature."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ed1ca8a996394b408b8766cfedbe46cb","ordinal":264,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.23","text":"\n\nDemocritus says that a human being is what we all know. As far as this goes, we will not know the human being. We also know a dog, and on that ground the dog too will be a human being. And there are some human beings we do not know, so they will not be human beings. Or rather, as far as this concept goes, no one will be a human being. He says that a human being must be known by everyone, and no human being is known by all human beings, so on his view no one will be a human being."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-cd086402a4254804871098b02f686bde","ordinal":265,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.24","text":"\n\nThat we are not arguing sophistically here is apparent from what follows from his position. The man says that only atoms and the void truly exist. He says that these belong not only to animals but to all compounds. So as far as these go, we will not conceive what is peculiar to the human being, since they are common to everything. But nothing else exists besides these. So we will have nothing by which we can distinguish the human being from the other animals and conceive him clearly."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7df2f3a379934006b30502cb6640fa50","ordinal":266,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.25","text":"\n\nEpicurus says that a human being is this sort of shape, endowed with soul. On his view too, since a human being is exhibited by pointing, anyone who is not pointed out is not a human being. If someone points out a woman, a man will not be a human being; if he points out a man, a woman will not be one. We will argue the same way from the difference of circumstances, which we know from the fourth mode of suspension."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-19ec636490be40089508488f62c71f8e","ordinal":267,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.26","text":"\n\nOthers have said that a human being is a rational mortal animal, receptive of mind and knowledge. But the first mode of suspension shows that no animal is non-rational, and that all animals are receptive of mind and knowledge, as far as their own statements go. So we will not know what in the world they mean."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-94cbc48432364956946f7924db52d876","ordinal":268,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.27","text":"\n\nMoreover, they mean the attributes included in the definition either in actuality or in potentiality. If in actuality, no one is a human being who has not already acquired complete knowledge, is perfect in reason, and is in the very act of dying, since that is what being mortal in actuality is. If in potentiality, then neither someone with perfect reason nor someone who has acquired mind and knowledge will be a human being, and this is more absurd than the first. So in this way too the conception of the human being has turned out to be incoherent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1486f295e6ea49d0bc265b89eeecae4d","ordinal":269,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.28","text":"\n\nPlato declares that a human being is a featherless, two-footed, flat-nailed animal, receptive of political knowledge. But not even he claims to set this out with firm affirmation. On his view the human being too is one of the things that come to be but never really are, and on his view it is impossible to make firm assertions about things that never are. So Plato will not want it to seem that he sets out the definition as a firm affirmation. He is speaking, as he usually does, in terms of what is persuasive."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1e6de4d65cd34ef3bc5e65218b522f6e","ordinal":270,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.29","text":"\n\nEven if we were to grant, for the sake of argument, that the human being can be conceived, he will be found to be inapprehensible. He is composed of soul and body, and perhaps neither the body nor the soul is apprehended. So neither is the human being."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-309d96ebe5bc45daa4f4753b5ee2eb58","ordinal":271,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.30","text":"\n\nThat the body is not apprehended is clear from this. The attributes of something are different from what they belong to. So when a color or something of the sort strikes us, it is likely that the attributes of the body strike us, not the body itself. They say, at any rate, that the body is extended in three dimensions. So to apprehend the body we ought to apprehend its length, breadth and depth. If that struck us, we would also recognize gilded silver coins for what they are. So the body is not apprehended either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4fbeb281e4a04bfaafb8b2eceb71c934","ordinal":272,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.31","text":"\n\nAnd even if we set aside the dispute about the body, the human being is again found to be inapprehensible, because the soul is inapprehensible. That the soul is inapprehensible is clear from this. Leaving aside the extensive and endless conflict, among those who have discussed the soul some said that there is no soul, like Dicaearchus of Messene and his followers. Others said that there is one, and others suspended judgment."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-84e3bc11467c40e6bae5e5010e4c2d16","ordinal":273,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.32","text":"\n\nIf the dogmatists say that this disagreement is undecidable, they will grant at once that the soul is inapprehensible. If they say it is decidable, let them tell us what they will decide it by. They cannot decide it by perception, since they say the soul is an object of thought. Suppose they say they will decide it by the intellect. Then we will say that the intellect is the most non-evident part of the soul, as is shown by those who agree about the soul’s existence but differ about the intellect. So if, by means of the"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c9b02baad9624d3693a2d9b96b35fa6a","ordinal":274,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.33","text":"\n\nintellect, they want to apprehend the soul and decide the disagreement about it, they will be trying to decide and confirm what is less in question by what is more in question, and that is absurd. So the disagreement about the soul will not be decided by the intellect either. It will be decided by nothing, then. And if so, the soul is inapprehensible too. Hence the human being would not be apprehended either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-117353c049c647449e13084f239b3180","ordinal":275,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.34","text":"\n\nEven if we grant that the human being is apprehended, it may well be impossible to show that things ought to be judged by him. Anyone who says that things ought to be judged by a human being will say it either without proof or with proof. He cannot say it with proof, because the proof must be true and judged, and therefore judged by something. We cannot say in an agreed way what the proof itself can be judged by, since we are investigating the criterion “by which.” So we will not be able to decide the proof, and therefore we will not be able to prove the criterion under discussion either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a575f14d2dfd4da5914f38aba55cd03b","ordinal":276,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.35","text":"\n\nIf it is said without proof that things ought to be judged by the human being, it will not be credible. So we will not be able to affirm firmly that the criterion “by which” is the human being. And what will judge that the criterion “by which” is the human being? They surely won’t be believed if they say this without judgment. If it is judged by a human being, the very point under investigation will be taken for granted."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4153e0cdb1824a0d92a45bc429011de7","ordinal":277,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.36","text":"\n\nIf it is judged by another animal, how is that animal brought in to judge that the criterion is a human being? If without judgment, it will not be believed. If with judgment, it in turn must be judged by something. If it is judged by itself, the same absurdity remains, for what is under investigation will be judged by what is under investigation. If it is judged by a human being, the mode of reciprocity comes in. If it is judged by something else besides these, we will again demand the criterion “by which” for that, and so on to infinity. For this reason too, then, we will not be able to say that things ought to be judged by a human being."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-31a54120bf0947e8bbae787cf43ebfa3","ordinal":278,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.37","text":"\n\nBut let it be so, and let it be taken on trust that things ought to be judged by the human being. Well then, since there is great difference among human beings, let the dogmatists first agree that this particular human being is the one we should attend to, and only then tell us to assent to him too. But if they are going to disagree about him “as long as water flows and tall trees flourish,” as the saying goes, how can they press us to assent rashly to anyone?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8a382768f6574c16a9db4082b398c5ed","ordinal":279,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.38","text":"\n\nIf they say that one must trust the wise man, we will ask them which wise man. Epicurus’ wise man, the Stoics’, ⟨the Cyrenaic, or⟩ the Cynic? They will not be able to say."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fdf2776bc70d4450943af92aeb36bd64","ordinal":280,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.39","text":"\n\nSomeone might ask us to give up the investigation about the wise man and simply trust the most intelligent of everyone alive. First, they will disagree about who is more intelligent than the rest. Then, even if it were granted that it could be agreed who is more intelligent than everyone who is and has been, not even so will he deserve to be trusted."}]}